Ownership is the third WHO axis, and an accepted decision's condition has been met

Design-only, two brainstorm Decisions at `proposed`, nothing promoted.

⭐ THE HEADLINE IS NOT THE ASK — IT IS THAT AN ACCEPTED DECISION SET A CONDITION
AND THE CONDITION HAS NOW BEEN MET BY EXACTLY THE EVIDENCE IT NAMED.

dec:ownership-reads-claims-before-adding-an-edge (accepted, Anthony 2026-08-08)
says: build the first cut from CLAIMS, and "decide on OWNED_BY only after that is
built and SHOWN TO BE INSUFFICIENT". Its rationale named the disqualifying
evidence in advance — "if claims turn out to model the wrong thing (they are
TRANSIENT WORK-IN-HAND, while ownership is DURABLE and survives the session),
that is a real distinction and the edge becomes justified on evidence rather
than on anticipation."

One day later dev_storyflow filed precisely that distinction, and Anthony
supplied the use case himself: "areas of the design that are his and areas that
are mine... durable, survives every session, and should never be released."

THE FRAMING — reflow2 has two of the three WHO axes:
  AUTHORED_BY  who WROTE this          past · historical · never changes
  CLAIMS       who is IN it RIGHT NOW  transient · advisory · released at checkout
  ???          whose AREA this is      durable · standing · never released

Why CLAIMS cannot be stretched, operationally rather than theoretically: its own
description says advisory and session-scoped, the skill says release at
checkout, and on a shared server a claim reads `unknown` and never expires. So
standing ownership claims would permanently drown the tool's actual job.

⚠️ AND THE FLEET RETRACTED ITS OWN COUNTER-ARGUMENT, which is worth more than the
ask: their objection (ownership in the graph duplicates their PROTOCOL.md matrix
and would rot) is "specific to an organisation that ALREADY writes ownership
down. A pair of collaborators has no matrix." For most projects the graph would
be the FIRST place ownership was recorded, not the second.

⭐ AND THE REFRAME THAT DECIDES SCOPE: every earlier version of this ask was
framed around an agent fleet, which invites "that is exotica". Two people
splitting a design is the ORDINARY case of collaborative design work.

HONEST CORRECTION RECORDED IN THE NODE: loop_status(contributor_id) shipped today
is NOT the claims-based first cut that decision specified — it attributes via the
approver edge. That was already recorded honestly on its SATISFIES edge as
`coverage: partial`. So the claims-based cut was never built, and whether it
still should be is one of the two readings the node lays out.

SECOND DECISION — dec:idea-detect-ownership-orphans. A different axis from
orphan_node: not structurally disconnected, but well-connected, realized,
verified, SHIPPING, and nobody accountable. A node can be structurally perfect
and governance-orphaned, which is more dangerous precisely because nothing about
it looks wrong.

⭐ AND IT IS THE THIRD MEMBER OF ONE FAMILY FOUND IN A SINGLE DAY:
  built, and nothing CONSUMES it   (req:the-design-says-what-was-built-and-never-used)
  declared, and nothing READS it   (req:a-vocabulary-distinction-proves-it-is-read)
  shipping, and nobody OWNS it     (this one)
Every existing gap asks what is missing FROM a node. All three ask what is
missing AROUND one, and none of the seven capability gaps can express that shape.

⚠️ THE HARD PART, recorded as a design input rather than a setting, and it is the
fleet's strongest objection: WHICH NODE TYPES ARE OWNABLE? In their 1,127-node
graph most nodes legitimately have no owner, so a naive detector fires on the
majority and becomes a signal trained to be ignored. In a two-person project the
design is mostly partitioned. Ownability looks like a per-project declaration.

reflow2_check: OK.

